<p><strong>Topological sort</strong><br>
The process we described above is essentially topological sorting, where ve=
rtices of a graph are sorted in such a way that for every directed edge u =
=E2=86=92 v, node u comes before node v in the ordering of the topological =
sort.</p>
<p>An algorithm designed to perform topological sort is <strong>Kahn=E2=80=
=99s algorithm</strong>. Let=E2=80=99s see how we can use it to solve this =
problem.</p>
<hr>
<p>The first step of Kahn's algorithm is to determine the in-degree of each=
 course. This can be achieved by counting the number of times each course a=
ppears as a dependent in the prerequisite pairs: for a pair [a, b], course =
b depends on course a, so course b=E2=80=99s in-degree is incremented by on=
e:</p>
